<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Sobukwe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Robert Mangaliso Sobukwe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Sobukwe was a South African anti-apartheid leader, a founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a> and, from 1959, its first president. A lecturer at the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>, he held that black South Africans must &quot;liberate themselves&quot; without the help of non-Africans. He launched the PAC's non-violent campaign against the pass laws in March 1960 and was jailed for three years for incitement. Under the &quot;Sobukwe Clause&quot; of 1963 he was held six more years in solitary confinement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a>, and from 1969 lived under house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-sobukwe.jpg" alt="Robert Sobukwe" width="301" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Sobukwe_&amp;_Benjamin_Pogrund_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
